{
  "result": "The old library on 5th Street has a chair that no one ever sits in. It's not roped off. There's no sign. It's just slightly too low, slightly too deep, upholstered in a green velvet that has gone shiny at the arms. I sit in it every Tuesday. \n\nI go there not to read, but to listen. Libraries have a very specific sound after you subtract the obvious. Not silence. A low hum of paper settling, radiators ticking, someone three aisles away trying not to cough. A student asleep with his cheek on a textbook. The librarian stamping dates even though the system is digital now \u2014 she likes the sound. \n\nLast Tuesday, a little girl asked me if I was the library's wizard. I was wearing a long grey cardigan that does look a bit wizardly. I told her yes, but only for lost things. She asked me to find her red hair tie. We looked under tables and inside the big atlas stand and couldn't find it. She left disappointed. \n\nAfter she left I found it around the leg of my green chair, holding it like a root. I kept it on my wrist. \n\nThis Tuesday I put it back on the lost and found shelf, but I looped it around a tiny origami crane I folded from a due date slip. If she comes back, she'll know the wizard remembered. \n\nLibraries keep much more than books. They keep time, folded small for anyone patient enough to wait still.",
